# RULED OUT. KEPT SO NOBODY SPENDS THE AFTERNOON ON IT AGAIN.
#
# Two attempts to give Nemotron Nano 9B v2 its own documented tool-calling
# format through a Modelfile. Both failed, for the same reason.
#
#   1. FROM the bartowski GGUF, which ships NVIDIA's real Jinja chat template
#      with <AVAILABLE_TOOLS>/<TOOLCALL>/<TOOL_RESPONSE> implemented correctly.
#      No effect.
#   2. An explicit TEMPLATE transcribing that same format, below. No effect.
#
# The measurement that settles it -- prompt tokens for one identical message,
# without and with a `tools` array:
#
#       nemotron-gpu      42 -> 619
#       this file         42 -> 619
#
# Byte-identical. **Ollama renders from its architecture-assigned RENDERER, and
# a Modelfile TEMPLATE does not displace it.** `/api/show` will happily report
# the template below, which is what made this look like it had worked; only the
# token count shows that nothing uses it.
#
# So the 9B keeps being shown the `nemotron-3-nano` format -- the 4B's, a
# different generation -- and improvises `conclude` as XML when the dialect it
# was given has no slot for it. Fixing that needs a change in Ollama or a
# different server, not a Modelfile.
#
# What was done instead: _calls_from_xml in M4's ollama_client.py recovers the
# shapes the model improvises, and every recovery is counted in `tool_recovered`
# per row so the cost stays visible.
#
# NVIDIA's format, transcribed from the GGUF's Jinja template. Kept because it
# is correct and will be needed if the renderer ever becomes selectable.
FROM nemotron-nano-9b-v2-q4kl:latest

TEMPLATE """<SPECIAL_10>System
{{- if .System }}
{{ .System }}
{{- end }}
{{- if .Tools }}
You can use the following tools to assist the user if required:
<AVAILABLE_TOOLS>[{{ range $i, $t := .Tools }}{{ if $i }}, {{ end }}{{ $t.Function }}{{ end }}]</AVAILABLE_TOOLS>

If you decide to call any tool(s), use the following format:
<TOOLCALL>[{"name": "tool_name1", "arguments": "tool_args1"}, {"name": "tool_name2", "arguments": "tool_args2"}]</TOOLCALL>

The user will execute tool-calls and return responses from tool(s) in this format:
<TOOL_RESPONSE>[{"tool_response1"}, {"tool_response2"}]</TOOL_RESPONSE>

Based on the tool responses, you can call additional tools if needed, correct tool calls if any errors are found, or just respond to the user.
{{- end }}
{{ range .Messages }}
{{- if eq .Role "user" }}<SPECIAL_11>User
{{ .Content }}
{{ else if eq .Role "tool" }}<SPECIAL_11>User
<TOOL_RESPONSE>[{{ .Content }}]</TOOL_RESPONSE>
{{ else if eq .Role "assistant" }}<SPECIAL_11>Assistant
{{ .Content }}
{{- if .ToolCalls }}
<TOOLCALL>[{{ range $i, $c := .ToolCalls }}{{ if $i }}, {{ end }}{"name": "{{ $c.Function.Name }}", "arguments": {{ $c.Function.Arguments }}}{{ end }}]</TOOLCALL>
{{- end }}
<SPECIAL_12>
{{ end }}
{{- end }}<SPECIAL_11>Assistant
<think>"""

PARAMETER stop "<SPECIAL_12>"
PARAMETER stop "<SPECIAL_11>"
PARAMETER stop "<SPECIAL_10>"
PARAMETER num_gpu 99
PARAMETER num_ctx 8192
